        <div id="charges" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Many iPhone Charges Is a 10,000mAh Power Bank? About 1.8
          </h2>

          <p>
            The number on the box is not the number of charges you get, and the gap trips up almost every iPhone owner. A power bank is rated in milliamp-hours at its internal cell voltage, about 3.6 to 3.85 volts, while it charges your phone through USB at 5 to 9 volts and then the phone stores the energy back at cell voltage. Every conversion step loses some energy as heat. The reliable way to estimate is to convert everything to watt-hours, apply a realistic efficiency, and divide by the phone's battery size. These are estimates, not measurements, but they line up closely with the charge counts Anker and INIU print on their own listings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Convert mAh to Watt-Hours</h3>

          <p>
            Multiply the rated capacity by the cell voltage and divide by 1,000. A 10,000mAh bank at 3.7 volts holds 10,000 x 3.7 / 1,000 = 37 Wh. A 5,000mAh bank holds about 18.5 Wh, and a 20,000mAh bank about 74 Wh. Watt-hours are also the unit airlines use, so it is worth getting comfortable with them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Take Off the Conversion Loss</h3>

          <p>
            By cable, a good bank typically delivers around 60 to 70% of its rated energy into the phone's battery once you count voltage conversion in the bank, in the phone and in the cable. Wireless loses more, because the magnetic coils turn part of the energy into heat, so plan on roughly 50 to 60% for a magnetic pack. Using 65% for a cable, the 10,000mAh bank delivers about 24 Wh; wirelessly at 55% it delivers about 20 Wh.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Divide by Your iPhone's Battery</h3>

          <p>
            A base iPhone 15 or 16 has a battery of roughly 13 Wh, a Pro model about 13 to 14 Wh, and a Pro Max around 17 to 18 Wh, based on commonly reported capacities. So 24 Wh divided by 13 Wh is about 1.8 charges by cable for a standard iPhone, which is exactly the figure Anker quotes for the iPhone 15 Pro. Wirelessly it is closer to 1.5. For a Pro Max, the same 10,000mAh bank gives about 1.3 charges by cable and a bit over one wirelessly.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Estimated iPhone charges (standard iPhone, ~13 Wh)</p>
