              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~1.5</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">10,000mAh magnetic</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~3.7</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">20,000mAh by cable</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Your Real Number Varies</h3>

          <p>
            Heat, a thick case on a magnetic pack, an old battery that holds less, and using the phone while it charges all shift the result. Treat the math as a planning figure and round down rather than up. If the estimate says 1.8, plan for one and a half.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="magsafe" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. MagSafe, Qi2 or Cable? Magnetic Is Convenient, Cable Is Efficient
          </h2>

          <p>
            The biggest choice is not the brand but how the bank connects. Magnetic banks snap onto the back of an iPhone 12 or later and charge without a cable. Cable banks plug into the port. Each has a clear job, and the right answer depends on whether you value convenience or charge per ounce.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">MagSafe-Compatible and Qi2 (15W)</h3>

          <p>
            Apple's MagSafe uses a ring of magnets to align the phone with the charging coil. Qi2 is the open standard built on that same magnetic design, and a Qi2 certified bank charges a compatible iPhone at up to 15W. Older packs sold as merely MagSafe-compatible often deliver only 7.5W to an iPhone, so look for Qi2 certification on the listing. The slim Anker MagGo, the Belkin BoostCharge Pro and the Baseus PicoGo are all Qi2 certified.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Qi2.2 (25W): The New Fast Lane</h3>

          <p>
            Qi2.2, sometimes marketed as Qi2 25W, raises magnetic charging to 25W on iPhones that support it, which Apple added for recent models. On older iPhones a Qi2.2 bank simply falls back to a lower speed. The Anker MagGo 2 Pro is the Qi2.2 pick here, and it adds a cooling fan because heat is what throttles wireless charging in practice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">USB-C Cable Banks</h3>

          <p>
            A cable bank sends power through the port, which wastes less energy and charges faster. A 20W or 30W cable bank beats a 15W magnetic one on speed and gives you more charges from the same capacity. You give up the snap-on convenience and have to hold two objects, or keep the phone tethered in a bag.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which One to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            If you top up during the day while using the phone, buy magnetic. If you want the most charges for a trip or a power cut, buy a cable bank or a magnetic bank with a strong USB-C port, like the slim MagGo, and use the cable when it matters. Here are the seven picks at a glance.
          </p>

