        <div id="watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 20W or 30W? 20W Fast-Charges Any iPhone, 30W Adds Headroom
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wattage tells you how fast a bank can push power. For an iPhone, the answer is simpler than the marketing suggests: 20W USB Power Delivery is the speed that unlocks fast charging, and anything above it is headroom rather than a big jump in speed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What an iPhone Actually Draws</h3>

          <p>
            Apple describes fast charging as reaching about 50% in roughly 30 minutes with a 20W or higher USB-C Power Delivery adapter. Recent models can briefly draw more than 20W early in the charge, then taper as the battery fills. A 30W port lets those phones run at their peak for longer and leaves room to charge AirPods at the same time without slowing the phone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power Delivery Is the Part That Matters</h3>

          <p>
            The protocol matters as much as the number. iPhones fast-charge over USB Power Delivery, so a bank advertising 22.5W through a different fast-charge standard may hand an iPhone much less. Every cable bank in this guide lists PD or a USB-C output, and the INIU states 20W PD outright. A USB-A port charges an iPhone at a slow trickle, so use USB-C.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shared Ports Split the Watts</h3>

          <p>
            Plug two devices into a bank and the total output is shared, often unevenly. If your iPhone suddenly charges slowly, unplug the second device. This matters most on the smaller banks, where a 20W total ceiling can drop to 15W or less per port once two devices are connected.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recharging the Bank Itself</h3>

          <p>
            Input wattage decides how long the bank takes to refill overnight or between flights. The MagGo 2 Pro lists 45W input; most compact banks accept 20W to 30W. Pair the bank with a wall charger at least as strong as its input rating, or a 10,000mAh pack can take most of an evening to refill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="connector" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. USB-C or Lightning? iPhone 15 and Later Use USB-C
          </h2>

          <p>
            Apple switched the iPhone from Lightning to USB-C with the iPhone 15 in 2023. That one change decides which cables and built-in connectors work for you, and it is the most common reason people buy the wrong bank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">iPhone 15 and Newer</h3>

          <p>
            These phones charge from any USB-C bank with a USB-C to USB-C cable, the same type Apple puts in the box. A plug-in bank like the Anker Nano works directly. You can also charge AirPods with a USB-C case from the same cable, which simplifies packing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">iPhone 14 and Older</h3>

          <p>
            These use Lightning, so you need a USB-C to Lightning cable from a USB-C bank, or a bank with a Lightning lead built in. Look for MFi certification on any Lightning cable: Apple's Made for iPhone program is what keeps a phone from rejecting the accessory. Every iPhone from the 12 onward also supports MagSafe, so a Qi2 magnetic bank skips the connector question entirely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Households With Both</h3>

          <p>
            If the family has Lightning and USB-C phones, a bank with both cables built in, like the Anker Zolo, ends the hunt for the right lead. The alternative is a magnetic bank for the iPhone 12 and newer phones, which charges both generations without any cable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Size Power Bank for an iPhone? 5,000mAh for a Day, 10,000mAh for a Weekend
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pick capacity by how long you will be away from an outlet and which iPhone you carry. Bigger is not automatically better: a heavier bank is the one that gets left at home.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5,000mAh: The Daily Top-Up</h3>

          <p>
            Roughly 70 to 90% of a standard iPhone, a little less wirelessly, and around half a Pro Max. Right for commuters who start the day near full and just need to get through the evening.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">10,000mAh: The Sweet Spot</h3>

          <p>
            About 1.5 to 1.8 standard iPhone charges, or a full charge and change for a Pro Max. This is the right size for most people and most trips, and it is still slim enough to clip to the phone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">20,000mAh: Trips, Families and Outages</h3>

          <p>
            Around 3.5 to 4 iPhone charges by cable, enough to keep two phones going over a weekend or a long power cut. It is too heavy to hang off the phone, so it lives in a bag or on a table. If you need even more, our guide to <a href="/best-portable-charger-for-power-outages/">portable chargers for power outages</a> covers banks and small stations built for multi-day use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pro Max Owners: Size Up One Step</h3>

          <p>
            A Pro Max battery is about 35% larger than a standard iPhone's, so a 5,000mAh pack that nearly fills a base model gives a Pro Max barely half. Pro Max owners should treat 10,000mAh as the minimum.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="airline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Can You Take an iPhone Power Bank on a Plane? Yes, Under 100 Wh
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every bank in this guide is well inside the airline limit. The rules are simple once you think in watt-hours rather than milliamp-hours, and they matter more each year as airlines tighten how power banks are carried and used on board.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 100 Wh Rule</h3>

          <p>
            Under FAA and IATA guidance, lithium-ion power banks up to 100 Wh can be carried without airline approval. Banks from 100 to 160 Wh need the airline's approval, and anything above 160 Wh is not allowed on passenger aircraft. A 20,000mAh bank at 3.7 volts is about 74 Wh, so even the largest pick here is comfortably allowed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carry-On Only</h3>

          <p>
            Spare lithium batteries, including power banks, must go in carry-on bags, never in checked luggage. If a gate agent tags your carry-on for the hold, take the bank out first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">In-Flight Rules Are Tightening</h3>

          <p>
            A growing number of airlines now restrict charging from power banks during flight or require them to be kept where you can see them rather than in an overhead bin. Check your carrier's current policy before you fly. For longer trips, our guide to <a href="/best-power-bank-for-travel/">travel power banks</a> covers airline rules in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="battery-health" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Will a Power Bank Hurt Your iPhone Battery? No, Heat Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            A quality power bank charges an iPhone the same way a wall adapter does, and the phone manages its own charging. The real enemy of battery health is heat, and that is where your habits matter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat Is the Variable to Watch</h3>

          <p>
            Wireless charging makes more heat than a cable, and stacking a magnetic bank on a phone in a hot car or in direct sun compounds it. The iPhone protects itself by slowing or pausing charging when it gets warm, which is why a magnetic bank can seem to stall on a summer afternoon. Take the phone out of the sun, or switch to a cable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Let Optimized Charging Do Its Job</h3>

          <p>
            iPhones include Optimized Battery Charging, and recent models let you set an 80% charge limit. Both reduce wear. If your phone stops at 80% while attached to a bank, that is the setting working, not the bank failing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Store the Bank Sensibly</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium banks age fastest when stored full and hot. For a bank kept in a drawer for emergencies, leave it around half charged and top it up every few months. Never charge a swollen, cracked or very hot bank; recycle it instead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cases and Magnetic Banks</h3>

          <p>
            Thick or non-magnetic cases weaken the hold and push the coil away from the phone, which lowers efficiency and raises heat. Use a MagSafe-compatible case with a magnetic ring, or a thin one, if you rely on a snap-on bank.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Keeping an iPhone Charged in an Outage: Bank First, Solar Second
          </h2>

          <p>
            When the power goes out, your iPhone becomes your flashlight, radio and only link to updates, and it drains faster than usual as it hunts for signal. A charged power bank is the simplest backup, and it pays to plan the way you use it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stretch the Phone First</h3>

          <p>
            Turn on Low Power Mode, dim the screen and close apps that use location. Every watt-hour you save on the phone is one the bank does not have to supply, and it often doubles how long a small bank keeps you connected.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use Cable, Not Magnet</h3>

          <p>
            In an outage, efficiency beats convenience. Charge by cable from the USB-C port rather than the magnetic pad and you get noticeably more charges from the same bank, as the math earlier shows.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Add Solar</h3>

          <p>
            A bank only empties during a long outage. If you live where outages run for days, add a way to refill it. Our <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">solar power bank guide</a> covers banks and folding panels that recharge from the sun, and it is the right next read for anyone planning for a multi-day blackout with an iPhone in the house. For a whole family's phones, tablets and a router, step up to a <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by mAh Without Doing the Watt-Hour Math</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 10,000mAh bank does not hold two and a half iPhone charges just because the phone's battery is about 3,500mAh. Conversion losses and cell voltage cut the real figure to about 1.8 by cable and 1.5 wirelessly for a standard iPhone. Convert to watt-hours, apply 55 to 65% efficiency and divide by your phone's battery before choosing a size.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Settling for 7.5W MagSafe-Compatible Packs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many cheap magnetic banks are labelled MagSafe-compatible but charge an iPhone at only 7.5W, which barely outpaces heavy use. Look for Qi2 certification on the listing, which guarantees up to 15W on supported iPhones, or Qi2.2 for 25W on recent models. The price gap is small and the speed difference is large.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging Wirelessly When Every Percent Counts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Magnetic charging is convenient but wastes more energy as heat. In a power cut, on a long hike or late in a travel day, plug in by cable from the bank's USB-C port instead. You will get noticeably more charge out of the same bank, and the phone stays cooler, which keeps it charging at full speed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Lightning to USB-C Switch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The iPhone 15 and newer use USB-C, while the iPhone 14 and older use Lightning. Buying a plug-in USB-C bank for an iPhone 13 or a Lightning-only bank for an iPhone 16 leaves you with a pack you cannot use directly. Check your model, and in a mixed household choose a bank with both cables or a magnetic one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Packing the Power Bank in Checked Luggage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Power banks must travel in carry-on bags under FAA and IATA rules, never in checked luggage. Every bank here is under the 100 Wh limit, but if a gate agent checks your carry-on, take the bank out first. Some airlines now also restrict using banks in flight, so check your carrier before boarding.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

