        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best power bank for iPhone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Anker MagGo Power Bank (10K, Slim). It is Qi2 certified for 15W magnetic charging, has a 30W USB-C port for faster wired top-ups, is rated for about 1.8 charges of an iPhone 15 Pro, and is slim enough for a pocket. Owners of recent iPhones that support Qi2.2 should consider the 25W Anker MagGo 2 Pro instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many times will a 10,000mAh power bank charge an iPhone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 1.8 times by cable for a standard iPhone, and roughly 1.5 times wirelessly. The math: 10,000mAh at 3.7 volts is 37 Wh, about 65% of which reaches the phone by cable, or around 24 Wh. Divided by a roughly 13 Wh iPhone battery, that is 1.8 charges. A Pro Max gets about 1.3 charges from the same bank.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a MagSafe power bank worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you top up while using your phone. A Qi2 magnetic bank snaps on, charges without a cable and lets you keep scrolling or navigating. It is less efficient than a cable, so you get fewer charges per mAh and more heat. If you mostly charge overnight or in an outage, a USB-C cable bank gives more charge for the money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between MagSafe and Qi2?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                MagSafe is Apple's magnetic charging system for iPhone 12 and later. Qi2 is the open wireless standard built on the same magnetic alignment, and a Qi2 certified bank charges a compatible iPhone at up to 15W. Qi2.2 raises that to 25W on recent iPhones that support it. Older packs sold only as MagSafe-compatible often deliver just 7.5W.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 20W or 30W power bank for iPhone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 20W USB Power Delivery output is enough to fast-charge any iPhone from the iPhone 8 on, reaching around 50% in about 30 minutes. A 30W port adds headroom so recent models can hold their peak charging rate longer and so a second device can charge at the same time without slowing the phone. Make sure the output is USB-C PD.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I take an iPhone power bank on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Power banks up to 100 Wh can be carried without airline approval under FAA and IATA guidance, and every bank in this guide is well below that; a 20,000mAh bank is about 74 Wh. They must go in carry-on luggage, never checked bags. Some airlines now restrict using power banks during the flight, so check your carrier's rules.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a power bank damage my iPhone battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A quality bank charges an iPhone the same way a wall adapter does, and the phone controls its own charging. Heat is what wears a battery, and wireless charging creates more of it. Avoid charging a phone in direct sun or a hot car with a magnetic bank attached, and let Optimized Battery Charging or the 80% limit work.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
